1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,124
April 2022.

2
00:00:01,124 --> 00:00:05,472
For the first time in ten years, Netflix lost subscribers.

3
00:00:05,472 --> 00:00:08,919
Two hundred thousand of them, in three months,

4
00:00:08,919 --> 00:00:12,517
and it warned that two million more could leave.

5
00:00:12,517 --> 00:00:15,140
Streaming, people said, had peaked.

6
00:00:15,590 --> 00:00:17,964
Fast forward to the summer of 2026.

7
00:00:17,964 --> 00:00:22,576
In three months, Netflix brought in almost thirteen billion dollars.

8
00:00:22,576 --> 00:00:28,070
And out of every dollar, it kept thirty-four point six cents as operating profit.

9
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:30,442
So what happened in between?

10
00:00:30,442 --> 00:00:32,845
Netflix didn't find a new business.

11
00:00:32,845 --> 00:00:37,651
It still sells a monthly subscription to a library of shows and films.

12
00:00:37,651 --> 00:00:43,280
But it found a handful of ways to squeeze far more money out of that same library.

13
00:00:43,730 --> 00:00:49,203
In this video, we take Netflix's latest accounts apart, dollar by dollar:

14
00:00:49,203 --> 00:00:52,501
where each dollar comes from, where it goes,

15
00:00:52,501 --> 00:00:55,650
and how the part that's left grew so fast.

16
00:00:59,250 --> 00:01:02,314
Let's start with the latest numbers Netflix

17
00:01:02,314 --> 00:01:05,307
has published, for July to September 2026.

18
00:01:05,307 --> 00:01:10,438
Revenue: twelve point nine billion dollars, up twelve percent in a year.

19
00:01:10,438 --> 00:01:13,930
Operating profit: almost four and a half billion.

20
00:01:14,380 --> 00:01:19,750
About forty-four cents of each dollar came from the United States and Canada,

21
00:01:19,750 --> 00:01:23,586
and thirty-two from Europe, the Middle East and Africa.

22
00:01:23,586 --> 00:01:27,980
Latin America and Asia-Pacific brought about twelve cents each.

23
00:01:28,430 --> 00:01:31,133
Almost all of it comes from subscriptions.

24
00:01:31,133 --> 00:01:34,738
Netflix expects about two point seven billion dollars of

25
00:01:34,738 --> 00:01:38,343
advertising revenue this year, out of fifty-one billion.

26
00:01:38,343 --> 00:01:39,630
Around five percent.

27
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:42,677
Now, where does a Netflix dollar go?

28
00:01:42,677 --> 00:01:46,789
The biggest slice, almost thirty-three cents, is content:

29
00:01:46,789 --> 00:01:50,396
the cost of the shows and films, spread over time.

30
00:01:50,396 --> 00:01:56,240
We'll come back to how that spreading works, because it's key to the whole story.

31
00:01:56,690 --> 00:02:01,071
Another fourteen cents covers the rest of running the service:

32
00:02:01,071 --> 00:02:04,674
streaming the video, payment fees, and other costs.

33
00:02:04,674 --> 00:02:09,125
Then eight cents go to technology, six and a half to marketing,

34
00:02:09,125 --> 00:02:11,810
and three and a half to general costs.

35
00:02:12,260 --> 00:02:16,248
What's left is thirty-four point six cents of operating profit.

36
00:02:16,248 --> 00:02:21,060
After interest and taxes, about twenty-eight and a half cents of net profit.

37
00:02:21,510 --> 00:02:23,862
A third of every dollar, before tax.

38
00:02:23,862 --> 00:02:28,305
For a company that spends billions on television, that's remarkable.

39
00:02:28,305 --> 00:02:33,270
To see where it comes from, we need one simple fact about a television show.

40
00:02:34,370 --> 00:02:38,055
A show costs the same to make whether one

41
00:02:38,055 --> 00:02:42,189
person watches it or three hundred million do.

42
00:02:42,189 --> 00:02:48,570
Once the bill is paid, every extra viewer costs Netflix almost nothing.

43
00:02:49,020 --> 00:02:52,812
That makes content something close to a fixed cost.

44
00:02:52,812 --> 00:02:56,009
And once a business covers its fixed costs,

45
00:02:56,009 --> 00:02:59,875
almost every extra dollar of revenue becomes profit.

46
00:02:59,875 --> 00:03:02,700
Economists call it operating leverage.

47
00:03:03,150 --> 00:03:06,095
Here's what that looks like at Netflix.

48
00:03:06,095 --> 00:03:09,795
In 2019, it had twenty billion dollars of revenue

49
00:03:09,795 --> 00:03:13,118
and a content expense of about nine billion.

50
00:03:13,118 --> 00:03:16,818
By 2025, revenue had grown to forty-five billion.

51
00:03:16,818 --> 00:03:18,102
The content bill?

52
00:03:18,102 --> 00:03:19,310
Sixteen billion.

53
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:25,333
Revenue more than doubled, while content costs grew by about three quarters.

54
00:03:25,333 --> 00:03:30,027
Content went from forty-six cents of every dollar to thirty-six,

55
00:03:30,027 --> 00:03:32,960
and in the latest quarter, thirty-three.

56
00:03:33,410 --> 00:03:35,950
The operating margin followed.

57
00:03:35,950 --> 00:03:38,066
Thirteen percent in 2019.

58
00:03:38,066 --> 00:03:39,675
Twenty-one in 2023.

59
00:03:39,675 --> 00:03:42,300
Twenty-nine and a half in 2025.

60
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:47,210
And Netflix is aiming for thirty-one and a half this year.

61
00:03:47,660 --> 00:03:48,877
Put it another way.

62
00:03:48,877 --> 00:03:53,167
In 2025, by our estimate, the average membership brought in roughly

63
00:03:53,167 --> 00:03:57,649
twelve dollars a month, and about four dollars forty paid for content.

64
00:03:57,649 --> 00:04:02,260
The trick is to make the twelve dollars grow faster than the four forty.

65
00:04:02,710 --> 00:04:05,384
Netflix has four levers to do that.

66
00:04:05,384 --> 00:04:06,300
Charge more.

67
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:07,905
Make more people pay.

68
00:04:07,905 --> 00:04:08,592
Sell ads.

69
00:04:08,592 --> 00:04:12,870
And add something its library doesn't have: live events.

70
00:04:13,970 --> 00:04:16,576
The simplest lever is the price tag.

71
00:04:16,576 --> 00:04:20,630
In March 2026, Netflix raised its American prices again.

72
00:04:20,630 --> 00:04:26,060
The plan with ads went from seven ninety-nine to eight ninety-nine a month.

73
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:30,476
Standard, from seventeen ninety-nine to nineteen ninety-nine.

74
00:04:30,476 --> 00:04:33,010
Premium, to twenty-six ninety-nine.

75
00:04:33,460 --> 00:04:34,475
Did people leave?

76
00:04:34,475 --> 00:04:36,326
Not many, according to Netflix.

77
00:04:36,326 --> 00:04:41,164
It wrote that "member response to our 2026 price changes is consistent with prior

78
00:04:41,164 --> 00:04:46,180
adjustments and our expectations", and cancellations were lower than a year earlier.

79
00:04:46,630 --> 00:04:49,126
When Netflix adds two dollars to a plan,

80
00:04:49,126 --> 00:04:51,871
it doesn't have to make a single extra show.

81
00:04:51,871 --> 00:04:55,989
The content bill stays where it was, so apart from taxes and fees,

82
00:04:55,989 --> 00:04:59,046
those two dollars flow almost straight to profit.

83
00:04:59,046 --> 00:05:01,542
The balancing act is to keep the library

84
00:05:01,542 --> 00:05:04,350
strong enough that leaving feels like a loss.

85
00:05:05,450 --> 00:05:08,197
The second lever came out of that 2022 crisis.

86
00:05:08,197 --> 00:05:11,841
Netflix estimated that its two hundred and twenty-two million

87
00:05:11,841 --> 00:05:15,245
paying households were sharing their accounts with around

88
00:05:15,245 --> 00:05:18,530
one hundred million other households that paid nothing.

89
00:05:18,980 --> 00:05:22,014
In 2023, it rolled out "paid sharing".

90
00:05:22,014 --> 00:05:24,968
An account was tied to one household.

91
00:05:24,968 --> 00:05:28,801
Viewers outside it could open their own account,

92
00:05:28,801 --> 00:05:32,873
or be added as an "extra member" for a monthly fee.

93
00:05:32,873 --> 00:05:39,260
Today in the US, that's seven ninety-nine with ads, or nine ninety-nine without.

94
00:05:39,710 --> 00:05:42,083
People predicted an angry exodus.

95
00:05:42,083 --> 00:05:47,835
Instead, Netflix added about twenty-nine and a half million memberships in 2023.

96
00:05:47,835 --> 00:05:53,300
In 2024, a record forty-one million, reaching three hundred and two million.

97
00:05:53,300 --> 00:05:58,190
By the end of 2025, more than three hundred and twenty-five million.

98
00:05:58,640 --> 00:06:00,495
Then it stopped telling us.

99
00:06:00,495 --> 00:06:05,787
Since 2025, Netflix no longer publishes its subscriber numbers every quarter,

100
00:06:05,787 --> 00:06:09,360
and asks to be judged on revenue and profit instead.

101
00:06:09,810 --> 00:06:12,566
The crackdown fits the fixed-cost logic perfectly.

102
00:06:12,566 --> 00:06:16,699
Those new members watched shows the sharers were already watching for free.

103
00:06:16,699 --> 00:06:19,730
Netflix got paid for viewing it was already delivering.

104
00:06:20,830 --> 00:06:23,040
The third lever is advertising.

105
00:06:23,040 --> 00:06:26,961
In November 2022, Netflix, which had long resisted ads,

106
00:06:26,961 --> 00:06:31,310
launched a cheaper plan with commercials in twelve countries.

107
00:06:31,760 --> 00:06:32,982
It caught on fast.

108
00:06:32,982 --> 00:06:38,682
By the end of 2023, it accounted for forty percent of sign-ups where it was offered.

109
00:06:38,682 --> 00:06:41,600
A year later, more than fifty-five percent.

110
00:06:42,050 --> 00:06:43,696
The money took longer.

111
00:06:43,696 --> 00:06:49,455
Ad revenue more than doubled in 2025, to over one and a half billion dollars.

112
00:06:49,455 --> 00:06:53,046
For 2026, Netflix aimed for about three billion,

113
00:06:53,046 --> 00:06:58,132
then trimmed that to two point seven in October, blaming a "slightly

114
00:06:58,132 --> 00:07:02,770
slower than expected rollout of some ad product capabilities".

115
00:07:03,220 --> 00:07:07,158
Netflix tells advertisers its ad plan reaches more than two

116
00:07:07,158 --> 00:07:10,561
hundred and fifty million "monthly active viewers",

117
00:07:10,561 --> 00:07:15,700
a figure based on its own estimate of how many people live in each household.

118
00:07:16,150 --> 00:07:19,586
So why bother, if ads are only five percent of revenue?

119
00:07:19,586 --> 00:07:21,898
Because they solve a pricing problem.

120
00:07:21,898 --> 00:07:26,833
The ad plan keeps a cheap entry ticket, at eight ninety-nine, without giving up

121
00:07:26,833 --> 00:07:31,706
on revenue per viewer: the subscriber pays part, the advertisers pay the rest.

122
00:07:31,706 --> 00:07:36,391
And every price rise on the other plans nudges a few more people toward it.

123
00:07:36,391 --> 00:07:39,390
Next year, it arrives in fifteen more countries.

124
00:07:40,490 --> 00:07:42,146
Back to that content bill.

125
00:07:42,146 --> 00:07:47,303
When Netflix pays for a show, it doesn't count the whole cost on the day it pays.

126
00:07:47,303 --> 00:07:49,341
It records the show as an asset,

127
00:07:49,341 --> 00:07:52,906
then expenses it bit by bit from the month it comes out.

128
00:07:52,906 --> 00:07:54,180
That's amortization.

129
00:07:54,180 --> 00:07:57,937
Netflix front-loads it, because most viewing happens early:

130
00:07:57,937 --> 00:08:02,330
"over ninety percent" of a show's cost is expensed within four years.

131
00:08:02,780 --> 00:08:05,987
This creates a gap between profit and cash.

132
00:08:05,987 --> 00:08:11,505
In 2019, Netflix paid almost fourteen billion dollars in cash for content,

133
00:08:11,505 --> 00:08:16,427
while its accounts showed a content expense of about nine billion.

134
00:08:16,427 --> 00:08:18,515
On paper, it was profitable.

135
00:08:18,515 --> 00:08:24,033
In reality, it burned three point three billion dollars of cash that year,

136
00:08:24,033 --> 00:08:28,060
the worst in its history, and borrowed the difference.

137
00:08:28,510 --> 00:08:30,008
Then the gap closed.

138
00:08:30,008 --> 00:08:35,852
In 2020, Netflix said it no longer needed to borrow for day-to-day operations.

139
00:08:35,852 --> 00:08:40,122
By 2025, it spent nearly eighteen billion dollars in cash

140
00:08:40,122 --> 00:08:44,392
on content against sixteen point four billion of expense.

141
00:08:44,392 --> 00:08:47,388
Free cash flow: nine and a half billion.

142
00:08:47,388 --> 00:08:50,310
For 2026, Netflix expects about twelve.

143
00:08:50,760 --> 00:08:52,731
And that cash is coming back out.

144
00:08:52,731 --> 00:08:56,196
Last quarter alone, Netflix spent four point eight billion

145
00:08:56,196 --> 00:08:59,840
dollars buying back its own shares, its biggest quarter ever.

146
00:09:00,290 --> 00:09:03,566
One caveat on that thirty-four point six percent.

147
00:09:03,566 --> 00:09:09,181
Netflix says the quarter beat its forecast mostly because of the timing of expenses.

148
00:09:09,181 --> 00:09:13,259
And a year earlier, a Brazilian tax dispute cost it about six

149
00:09:13,259 --> 00:09:17,805
hundred and nineteen million dollars, which flatters the comparison.

150
00:09:17,805 --> 00:09:22,017
For the full year, the target is thirty-one and a half percent,

151
00:09:22,017 --> 00:09:24,825
and for the last quarter, just twenty-six.

152
00:09:24,825 --> 00:09:27,566
Why so much lower at the end of the year?

153
00:09:27,566 --> 00:09:29,170
That's the fourth lever.

154
00:09:30,270 --> 00:09:33,759
For most of its life, Netflix stayed away from live sports.

155
00:09:33,759 --> 00:09:38,076
Rights are expensive, and once a game is over, it's worth almost nothing.

156
00:09:38,076 --> 00:09:41,270
The opposite of a library show people watch for years.

157
00:09:41,720 --> 00:09:43,524
Then it changed its mind.

158
00:09:43,524 --> 00:09:48,142
In November 2024, the Jake Paul against Mike Tyson fight reached

159
00:09:48,142 --> 00:09:52,327
sixty-five million concurrent streams, by Netflix's count.

160
00:09:52,327 --> 00:09:56,440
A month later came its first NFL games, on Christmas Day.

161
00:09:56,890 --> 00:10:00,558
It signed a ten-year deal for WWE's weekly show Raw,

162
00:10:00,558 --> 00:10:03,168
worth more than five billion dollars.

163
00:10:03,168 --> 00:10:07,612
And this quarter is its "biggest quarterly live slate to date":

164
00:10:07,612 --> 00:10:11,422
the NFL's first Thanksgiving Eve game, a Christmas Day

165
00:10:11,422 --> 00:10:15,090
doubleheader, and Tyson Fury against Anthony Joshua.

166
00:10:15,540 --> 00:10:19,368
Why pay for something that loses value the moment it ends?

167
00:10:19,368 --> 00:10:23,197
Because live creates a date: you have to be there tonight,

168
00:10:23,197 --> 00:10:25,309
so people sign up, or come back.

169
00:10:25,309 --> 00:10:30,853
It gathers huge audiences at the same moment, exactly what advertisers pay most for.

170
00:10:30,853 --> 00:10:34,285
And a weekly show gives fans a reason not to cancel.

171
00:10:34,285 --> 00:10:36,860
It's the one lever that adds real cost,

172
00:10:36,860 --> 00:10:40,820
which is partly why the margin dips at the end of each year.

173
00:10:41,920 --> 00:10:43,295
Is it all smooth?

174
00:10:43,295 --> 00:10:44,104
Not quite.

175
00:10:44,104 --> 00:10:46,046
The first crack is time.

176
00:10:46,046 --> 00:10:51,062
In the second half of 2025, members watched ninety-six billion

177
00:10:51,062 --> 00:10:55,916
hours of Netflix, only two percent more than a year earlier.

178
00:10:55,916 --> 00:10:59,799
In October, Netflix wrote: "We know there's some

179
00:10:59,799 --> 00:11:03,520
investor concern about our view hours growth."

180
00:11:03,970 --> 00:11:07,090
The fixed-cost machine runs on one assumption:

181
00:11:07,090 --> 00:11:10,480
that people find enough value to keep paying more.

182
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:15,770
If viewing stalls while prices rise, the price lever eventually stops working.

183
00:11:16,220 --> 00:11:19,907
The second crack: ads are growing more slowly than planned,

184
00:11:19,907 --> 00:11:23,406
and this quarter Netflix is cutting jobs, a cost it says

185
00:11:23,406 --> 00:11:26,780
will knock more than a point off the quarter's margin.

186
00:11:27,230 --> 00:11:29,497
The third is a road not taken.

187
00:11:29,497 --> 00:11:33,502
In December 2025, Netflix agreed to buy Warner Bros.,

188
00:11:33,502 --> 00:11:37,659
at twenty-seven seventy-five per Warner share, in cash.

189
00:11:37,659 --> 00:11:39,850
Paramount Skydance outbid it.

190
00:11:39,850 --> 00:11:44,309
In February 2026, Warner ended the deal, and Paramount paid

191
00:11:44,309 --> 00:11:48,390
Netflix a two point eight billion dollar break-up fee.

192
00:11:48,840 --> 00:11:52,012
That fee inflates Netflix's net profit this year.

193
00:11:52,012 --> 00:11:57,321
But it also tells you something: even with more than three hundred and twenty-five

194
00:11:57,321 --> 00:11:59,976
million memberships, Netflix was ready to

195
00:11:59,976 --> 00:12:02,760
spend tens of billions to own more content.

196
00:12:03,860 --> 00:12:06,772
So, how does Netflix actually make money?

197
00:12:06,772 --> 00:12:12,667
It sells access to a library whose cost barely depends on how many people watch it.

198
00:12:12,667 --> 00:12:17,568
Then it stacks revenue on top: higher prices, households that used to

199
00:12:17,568 --> 00:12:22,540
share a password, advertisers, and live events that bring people back.

200
00:12:22,990 --> 00:12:27,382
The content bill still grows, by about ten percent this year.

201
00:12:27,382 --> 00:12:29,974
But as long as revenue grows faster,

202
00:12:29,974 --> 00:12:33,646
the gap keeps widening, and that gap is the profit.

203
00:12:33,646 --> 00:12:36,310
Thirteen cents on the dollar in 2019.

204
00:12:36,310 --> 00:12:37,750
About a third today.

205
00:12:38,200 --> 00:12:42,806
It all rests on one question: will people keep watching

206
00:12:42,806 --> 00:12:46,909
enough to accept paying a little more every year?

207
00:12:46,909 --> 00:12:47,914
So far, yes.

208
00:12:47,914 --> 00:12:51,683
That's what Netflix really sells: not a show,

209
00:12:51,683 --> 00:12:55,200
but the feeling you'd miss it if you left.
